/* 段落记忆 · 词层 6 · 子块 d：词头 → {tw:[关系词,关系类型], en, zh} */
window.APP_MERGE_NET(6,{"sub":"d","par":{"suffice":{"tw":[["sufficient",4],["answer",0],["qualify",3],["satisfy",2],["presume",6],["sufficiency",4],["serve",3],["flinch",6]],"en":"When my cousin applied for a job at the small bakery on our street, she worried that her experience would not suffice. The manager asked her to qualify her answer with an example, and she described two summers of early shifts in a hotel kitchen. That seemed sufficient to satisfy him, though he did not presume she could decorate a cake on her first day. He offered her a trial morning and watched her serve customers, weigh flour, and clean as she went. Nothing in her manner made him flinch, and the quiet sufficiency of her work spoke for itself. By noon he said the trial would serve as her training, and she started on Monday.","zh":"表姐去我们街上那家小面包房应聘时，担心自己的经验不够用。经理要她举个例子把回答说明白，她讲了在酒店厨房连着两个夏天上早班的经历。这似乎已足以让他满意，不过他并没有想当然地认为她第一天就会裱花。他让她试做一上午，看她招呼客人、称面粉、边做边收拾。她的举止没有一点让他皱眉退步的地方，那种安静而够用的踏实本身就说明了一切。中午他说试工就算作培训，她周一正式上班。"},"repel":{"tw":[["repulsive",4],["drive",0],["fight",2],["attract",1],["subdue",6],["disgust",3],["push",2],["bombard",6],["revolt",0]],"en":"My grandmother refuses to use strong sprays in her garden, so every summer she fights the insects with plants that repel them. She sets marigolds beside the beans, because their smell seems to drive the beetles away and even subdue the smaller ones. Other flowers attract bees, and she is careful to keep the two kinds apart. The first year a chemical wash left a repulsive film on the leaves, and the taste disgusted her so much that she threw the whole crop away. Now she would rather push a hose of plain water over the rows than bombard the soil with anything else. She says a garden should repel what damages it without revolting the person who eats from it.","zh":"奶奶不肯在园子里用强力杀虫剂，所以每年夏天她都靠一些能驱虫的植物来对付虫子。她在豆角旁边种万寿菊，那气味似乎能把甲虫赶走，连小一点的虫也能压制住。另一些花是用来招蜜蜂的，她小心地把两类分开种。头一年她用了一次药水，叶子上留下一层令人反感的薄膜，那味道让她恶心到把整茬菜都扔了。如今她宁愿拿水管在菜行上冲清水，也不愿往土里轰别的东西。她说，园子该把害它的东西挡在外面，而不该让吃菜的人反胃。"},"solicit":{"tw":[["solicitor",4],["court",0],["chase",3],["ask",2],["invite",6],["beg",0],["quest",3],["request",2],["woo",0]],"en":"The small library near the station was told last winter that its budget would be cut, so the staff began to solicit help from everyone they knew. A solicitor from a local firm offered free advice on how to register as a charity. Volunteers would chase shop owners for small donations, ask neighbours for old books, and invite students to run a reading hour. One retired teacher begged the council for a second hearing and wrote a formal request that took her a week. The librarian did not want to court publicity, but she agreed to a short piece in the local paper. Their quest raised enough for a new heating system, and nobody had to woo a single large donor.","zh":"车站旁那家小图书馆去年冬天被告知预算要削减，于是馆员们开始向所有认识的人求助。一位本地事务所的律师免费帮他们出主意，教他们怎么注册成慈善机构。志愿者们会挨家追着店主讨一点捐款，向邻居要旧书，还请学生来办一小时读书会。一位退休教师向议会恳求再给一次听证机会，写了一周的正式申请。馆长本不想招来媒体关注，但还是答应让本地报纸登一小篇。他们这一番奔走凑够了换新暖气的钱，而且没有去讨好任何一位大金主。"},"waver":{"tw":[["wave",4],["hesitate",2],["hover",3],["move",2],["relent",6],["weave",0],["voice",2],["impinge",6],["flicker",0]],"en":"For two weeks my sister's voice on the phone would waver whenever we talked about the job offer in another town. She would hesitate, then say she needed more time, and I could hear the doubt hover behind every sentence. Our parents did not want to impinge on her choice, so they said little, though my mother's opinion flickered in her eyes. On Sunday we sat in the garden and watched the wind weave through the tall grass while she finally voiced her real fear: that moving away meant losing us. Nobody tried to talk her out of it. A week later she relented and took the job, and she waved from the train with a steady hand.","zh":"那两个星期，一谈起外地那份工作，姐姐在电话里的声音就会发飘。她总是迟疑一下，说还要再想想，我能听出每个句子背后悬着的犹疑。父母不想干涉她的选择，所以话很少，可母亲的态度在她眼里一闪一闪。周日我们坐在院子里，看风在高草间穿来穿去，她终于说出真正的怕：搬走就意味着失去我们。没人劝她留下。一周后她松了口，接了那份工作，在火车上稳稳地朝我们挥手。"},"solvent":{"tw":[["solve",4],["answer",0],["medium",2],["insolvent",1],["liquid",6],["result",0],["soluble",6],["resolution",0]],"en":"At the print workshop where I spent a summer, every evening ended with the same job: cleaning the rollers with a sharp-smelling solvent. The foreman called the liquid the medium that carries the ink, and warned us that some inks are only soluble in the stronger kind. We would solve each stubborn mark by soaking the cloth and rubbing in small circles, and the result was a roller clean enough to shine. He kept the used solvent in a marked can, because the shop could not afford to be careless with it. The business itself had been insolvent two years before, he said, and the answer had been fewer jobs done better. That resolution saved the place.","zh":"我打过一个夏天工的那家印刷作坊，每天傍晚都以同一件事收尾：用一股刺鼻气味的溶剂清洗墨辊。工头把这种液体叫作承载油墨的介质，还提醒我们有些墨只能用更强的那种才溶得开。遇到顽固的印痕，我们就浸湿抹布，一圈圈地擦，最后墨辊干净得能反光。用过的溶剂他装在一只做了记号的桶里，因为这小厂经不起浪费。他说这生意两年前差点破产，办法就是少接活、把活做好。正是这个决断救了这家店。"},"rebound":{"tw":[["spring",0],["skip",3],["catch",2],["slide",6],["bounce",3],["resilience",3],["jump",2],["quarter",6],["rally",0]],"en":"After the flood closed the market for a month, the traders expected the town to take a year to rebound. Instead the stalls were back by the second week, and by the end of the quarter the square was busier than before. A child's ball would bounce off the wall of the old hall, and someone had painted a line for a game on the back wall. The baker said his sales were the first to spring back, and that jump gave the others hope. Even the flower seller stopped to rally the crowd with jokes. People learned to skip the long way round and slide past the scaffolding without complaint. What surprised me was the resilience of a place that had lost so much. The catch, of course, is that the river has not gone anywhere.","zh":"洪水让集市关了一个月，商户们原以为镇子要一年才能缓过来。结果第二周摊位就都回来了，到那个季度结束时，广场比从前还热闹。孩子的球会从老厅的墙上弹回来，后墙上不知谁画了一条球场的线。面包师说他的生意最先回升，那一涨给了其他人盼头。连卖花的也停下来，用笑话给大家打气。人们学会了绕远路时不多想，也学会了从脚手架旁边侧身而过而不抱怨。让我意外的是，一个损失这么大的地方竟有这般韧性。当然，麻烦在于那条河并没有走远。"},"recess":{"tw":[["break",0],["cove",3],["end",2],["convene",6],["corner",0],["alcove",3],["pause",2],["culminate",6],["recession",0]],"en":"My aunt runs a small hotel above a quiet cove, and every August the family gathers there for a week of repairs. The dining room has a deep recess in one wall, fitted out as an alcove with a bench and two lamps, and that is where we convene each morning. The talks run until noon, then we take a pause for soup; the break is short, because the list of jobs is long. Arguments usually start in the corner by the window and culminate in someone laughing at the size of the bill. This year a recession has cut the number of guests, so the meeting ended with a decision to close one floor. My aunt says a good recess in a wall, like a good rest in a day, makes a building easier to live in.","zh":"我姑妈在一处安静的小海湾上方经营一家小旅馆，每年八月全家都会去那儿住一周，帮着修缮。餐厅的墙上有一处很深的凹进，被布置成带长凳和两盏灯的小壁龛，我们每天早上就在那儿碰头。商量一直持续到中午，然后歇一歇喝汤；休息很短，因为要干的活列得很长。争执通常从窗边那个角落开始，最后总以有人笑账单太贵收场。今年经济不景气，客人少了许多，于是会议最后决定关掉一层楼。姑妈说，墙上有个好凹处，就像一天里有个好休息，会让整栋房子更好住。"},"regenerate":{"tw":[["reform",0],["replace",3],["better",2],["heal",6],["restore",3],["revive",3],["change",2],["nourish",6],["reclaim",0]],"en":"Three years ago the old factory by the river closed, and the town began to regenerate the land it left behind. Volunteers came first to reclaim the yard, pulling wire and broken glass out of the mud, and then a slow reform of the soil began. They replaced the cracked concrete with beds of dark earth and used compost from the market to nourish it, season after season. Grass and then small trees started to revive, and the river, which had been gray for years, began to heal along its bank. Children now cross the field to school, and the change is easy to see from the bridge. The aim was never to restore the place exactly as it was, but to better it, and to let the ground change at its own speed.","zh":"三年前河边的老厂子关门了，镇上开始修复它留下的这片地。最先来的是志愿者，他们把铁丝和碎玻璃从泥里清理出来，让这块地重新可用，接着是对土壤缓慢的改良。他们把开裂的水泥挖掉，换成一块块深色的土，又用市场拉来的堆肥一季一季地养它。先是草，然后是小树，慢慢活了过来，那条灰了许多年的河，岸边也开始慢慢愈合。现在孩子们穿这片地走去上学，从桥上就能看出变化。我们的目标从来不是把这里恢复成原样，而是让它变得更好，让土地按自己的速度慢慢改变。"},"relinquish":{"tw":[["quit",0],["discharge",3],["pass",2],["cede",6],["release",0],["disengage",3],["abandon",2],["vacate",6],["resign",0]],"en":"For forty years my uncle kept the keys to every door in the street, and this spring he finally agreed to relinquish the shop. He did not quit on a Monday morning; he spent six months teaching a young apprentice which key belonged to which lock. In June he began to vacate the back room, carrying home boxes of screws he would never use. He would discharge the last few orders himself, then pass the ledger to her and cede the counter without a word. It was harder than he expected to release his grip on the trade, and twice he nearly abandoned the plan and stayed on. When he finally signed the papers, he did not resign from the work so much as step back from it, and he disengaged slowly, one drawer at a time.","zh":"四十年里，我叔叔掌管着这条街每一扇门的钥匙，今年春天他终于答应把铺子交出去。他没有在某个周一早上忽然不干了；他花了半年时间，教一个年轻学徒哪把钥匙开哪把锁。六月他开始腾空后面的房间，把一箱箱再也用不上的螺丝搬回家。最后几单活他仍亲自做完，然后把账本交给那姑娘，一言不发地让出了柜台。放下这门手艺比他想的难，有两次他差点放弃这个计划，继续干下去。真正签字那天，他与其说是辞了这份活，不如说是从里面退了出来，一个抽屉一个抽屉地慢慢抽身。"},"renounce":{"tw":[["quit",0],["deny",3],["abandon",2],["abide",6],["resign",0],["swallow",3],["reject",2],["disavow",6],["relinquish",0]],"en":"When our grandmother died, she left one small house to three sisters, and by spring nobody could agree what to do with it. My eldest aunt wanted to keep it, the middle one wanted to sell, and my mother said little for weeks. In the end my mother decided to relinquish her share, and went to the lawyer to renounce it in person. She did not deny the house was worth money; she simply refused to let a roof break the family. It was hard for her to swallow the loss, and she would not abide another meeting full of raised voices. She abandoned the plan to buy out her sisters, rejected every hint that she was noble, and disavowed any talk of sacrifice. Then she quit her post as peacemaker, resigned from the argument, and went back to her garden.","zh":"外婆去世时留下一栋小房子，由三个姐妹分，可到了春天谁也说服不了谁。大姨想留着，二姨想卖掉，最小的那个——我母亲——好几个星期没说什么。最后母亲选择放弃自己那一份，好让另外两人把事情了结。她并不否认房子值钱，她只是不愿意让一层楼、一个屋顶把一家人拆开。咽下这份损失对她不容易，她也不愿再忍受一场满是高声的会议。谁说她高尚，她都一口回绝；谁提牺牲，她都断然不认；签字时也没搞什么仪式。之后她辞掉了家里和事佬的角色，回她的园子去了。"},"scorn":{"tw":[["despite",0],["sneer",3],["hate",2],["reject",0],["rebuff",3],["refuse",2],["dismay",6],["contempt",0]],"en":"My sister works in the back room of a family bakery, and last winter she suggested paper bags instead of thick plastic ones. The older baker met the idea with scorn: he said a paper bag would tear before the customer reached the corner, and he made no effort to hide his contempt. One assistant gave a small sneer, another refused to pack in anything but plastic, and the owner's first answer was a flat rebuff. My sister felt the dismay of a person who has prepared well and been laughed at, but she did not hate them for it. Despite the cold reception, she came in early for a week, packed bread in paper, and recorded every complaint. By Friday the bread was still warm and the bags were still whole, and the scorn had quietly turned into questions; nobody wanted to reject her idea now.","zh":"我姐姐在一家家庭面包房的后间干活，去年冬天她提议用纸袋代替那种厚塑料袋。老师傅对这主意满是不屑：他说纸袋还没等客人走到街角就破了，而且毫不掩饰自己的轻蔑。一个伙计轻轻哼了一声，另一个只肯用塑料袋装，老板的第一句回答也是干脆的回绝。姐姐心里那种备足了功课却被人取笑的失落很明显，但她并不因此恨他们。尽管反应冷淡，她还是提前一周每天早到，用纸袋装面包，把每一条抱怨都记下来。到周五，面包还是热的，袋子还是好的，那点不屑悄悄变成了问句；这时谁也不想再否决她的主意了。"},"slit":{"tw":[["scratch",0],["score",3],["cut",2],["knife",6],["slice",0],["slot",3],["crack",2],["snatch",0]],"en":"The parcel arrived on a wet Tuesday with a long slit along one side, as if someone had opened it and changed their mind. I carried it to the kitchen table, slid a knife under the tape, and cut a clean slice through the cardboard while my sister watched. She had ordered a lamp, and the shade came out scratched, with a fine scratch across the glass that looked like a hair. There was also a crack in the base, a thin score in the metal that no one had mentioned online. We photographed the damage, dropped the claim into the letter slot of the corner shop, and waited. The shop's dog snatched the wrapper from the step and ran off with it, which at least made us laugh. Two days later a new lamp came, unmarked and whole.","zh":"包裹是在一个下雨的周二到的，一侧有一道长长的裂口，像是有人拆开过又改了主意。我把它搬到厨房桌上，用刀尖挑开胶带，在纸板上划开一道整齐的口子，姐姐在旁边看着。她订的是一盏灯，灯罩上有划痕，玻璃上那道细细的擦伤像一根头发。底座也有一条裂缝，金属上有一道没人提过的浅刻。我们拍了照片，把索赔单投进街角小店的投信口，然后等。店里的狗把包装纸从台阶上叼走跑了，这倒让我们笑了一场。两天后新灯送到，完好无损。"},"snipe":{"tw":[["round",0],["rip",3],["hunt",2],["decoy",6],["attack",0],["knock",2],["squirrel",6]],"en":"Every spring a small group of us walks the same wet meadow before sunrise, hunting a glimpse of a snipe. My teacher carries a wooden decoy under his arm and sets it in the reeds, then we wait without speaking. The birds are hard to find: one round of the field may give nothing but a heron, and the wind will rip at our coats for an hour. When a snipe finally rises, it goes up fast and straight, and the whole party holds its breath. Midges attack us in clouds, and once a squirrel knocked a stick down from a willow and made everyone jump. My teacher says the trick is patience, not luck; the bird will come if the ground is quiet. We saw two that morning, and walked home with wet boots and cold hands.","zh":"每年春天，我们几个都会在日出前走同一片湿草地，想看一眼沙锥鸟。老师腋下夹着一只木制的诱鸟模型，把它放在芦苇里，然后大家不出声地等。这种鸟很难找：绕田走一圈，可能只见到一只苍鹭，风还能撕扯我们的外套整整一个钟头。等沙锥真的飞起来，它又快又直地窜上天，所有人都会屏住呼吸。小虫成群地扑上来，有一回一只松鼠从柳树上碰下一根树枝，把大家吓了一跳。老师说诀窍是耐心而不是运气，只要地面安静，鸟总会来。那天早上我们看见了两只，踩着湿靴子、冻着手走回了家。"},"stifle":{"tw":[["knee",0],["suffocate",3],["die",2],["stimulate",1],["restrain",6],["choke",0],["joint",2],["hinder",6],["repress",0]],"en":"Six weeks after my knee operation, the joint still felt stiff, and the tight bandage seemed to stifle it rather than help. My physiotherapist would restrain me when I tried to bend it too fast, and tell me to breathe instead of holding my breath until I nearly choked. The old habit of pushing through pain had to die, she said; fear only hinders the muscle she was trying to wake. So she attached two small pads that stimulate the thigh, and I lay there watching the numbers climb. On the bus home the heat and the crowd made me feel I might suffocate, and I had to repress the urge to stand up and shout. By August I could walk to the corner without thinking about it. The knee is not young again, but it works.","zh":"膝盖手术六周后，关节还是发僵，那条勒紧的绷带与其说在帮忙，不如说把关节憋住了。我想快点弯腿时，理疗师会按住我，让我呼吸，而不是屏着气直到差点噎住。她说，那种硬扛疼痛的老习惯必须彻底结束；恐惧只会妨碍她要唤醒的那块肌肉。于是她贴上两片小电极刺激大腿，我躺在那儿看着数字往上走。回家的公交上又热又挤，我觉得快要喘不上气，只能强压下站起来大喊的冲动。到八月，我已经能走到街角而不再惦记这条腿。膝盖没有恢复年轻，但它能用。"},"subdue":{"tw":[["master",0],["silence",3],["control",2],["repel",6],["reduce",0],["wink",3],["lower",2],["overpower",6],["overcome",0]],"en":"My cousin teaches riding at a small stable outside town, and she says the first lesson is always about fear. A young horse will test you for an hour, and if you try to overpower it, you will lose; you have to subdue it with a calm voice and slow hands. She would master a difficult animal in a week by doing almost nothing, standing in the ring until it lowered its head. When I rode a nervous mare last month, I had to control my breathing, repel the urge to pull the reins, and wink at my cousin to show I was fine. It took twenty minutes to reduce the animal's panic to a walk. She said we overcome fear the same way, and then she silenced the radio so we could hear the hooves on the sand.","zh":"我表姐在城外一个小马场教骑马，她说第一课永远是关于害怕。一匹年轻的马会试探你一个钟头，你若想用蛮力压住它，必输；得用平稳的嗓子和缓慢的手把它安定下来。她一周就能驯服一匹烈马，靠的几乎是什么都不做，只是站在围栏里，直到马低下头。上个月我骑一匹紧张的母马时，得控制自己的呼吸，压住拉缰绳的冲动，还朝表姐眨眨眼表示我没事。花了二十分钟，才把它的惊慌降成慢步。她说我们克服恐惧也是同样的法子，然后把收音机关了，好让我们听见沙地上的马蹄声。"},"tally":{"tw":[["add",0],["kick",3],["bill",2],["overall",6],["agree",0],["look",3],["record",2],["preliminary",6],["check",0]],"en":"On Saturday I kept the tally at a school football match, because the usual volunteer had gone to a wedding. My job was simple: watch the referee, add each goal to the sheet, and check the time. The first half was slow, with one kick that hit the post and nothing else, and the preliminary count at the break was still nil. Then a boy scored twice in four minutes, and the small crowd began to shout. At the end the two coaches did not agree about the second goal, so I had to look at my record again and read the numbers aloud. The overall score was three to one, and the bill for the tea and cakes came to less than the gate money. Nobody argued for long.","zh":"周六我在一场学校足球赛上记分，因为平时那个志愿者去参加婚礼了。我的活很简单：盯着裁判，把每个进球加到纸上，再看看时间。上半场很闷，只有一脚射门打在门柱上，别的什么都没有，中场休息时的初步统计还是零。后来一个男孩四分钟内进了两球，小小的人群开始喊起来。终场时两位教练对第二个进球意见不一，我只好又看了一遍记录，把数字念出来。总比分是三比一，茶和点心的账单比门票收入还少。没人争太久。"},"traverse":{"tw":[["cover",2],["course",3],["pass",2],["gorge",6],["cross",0],["drive",3],["travel",2],["route",6],["track",0]],"en":"The guidebook said the gorge could be traversed in a day, so we travelled light and started at six with two bottles of water each. The route follows the river for the first hour, then crosses a wooden bridge and climbs above the water. We had to cover eleven kilometers before noon, and the track was rough enough that we passed the same stone marker twice, certain we were lost. Below us the river had cut its course through the rock over thousands of years, and the walls were cool and dark. A man was driving a small cart of supplies along the far bank, the only vehicle for miles. We crossed the last stream at four, tired but pleased; the whole traverse took nine hours, and my legs still remember it.","zh":"旅行指南上说，这条峡谷一天就能走完，于是我们轻装上路，六点出发，每人带两瓶水。路线头一个小时沿着河走，然后过一座木桥，爬升到河面之上。我们得在中午前走完十一公里，小路很粗劣，同一个石标我们经过了两回，还以为自己迷了路。脚下那条河在几千年里把岩层切出一道河床，两岸的岩壁又冷又暗。对岸有个男人赶着一辆装补给的小车，那是方圆几里唯一的车辆。四点我们蹚过最后一道溪，累却高兴；整趟穿越用了九个小时，我的腿到现在还记得。"},"recede":{"tw":[["recession",4],["retire",0],["ebb",3],["regress",2],["advance",1],["retreat",0],["withdraw",0]],"en":"After the storm the sea climbed the beach road and pushed into the lower streets, and for two days the water did not recede. We watched it from the upstairs window: first the advance across the car park, then the slow ebb as the wind turned. On the third morning the tide retreated before noon, leaving a line of weed along every wall, and the volunteers began to withdraw from the shelter one by one. My father, who had planned to retire from the rescue team this year, stayed until the last street was clear. The town's small businesses had already been through a recession, and now they faced repairs they could not afford. Some things regress, he said, but water only goes back where it came from.","zh":"暴风雨过后，海水漫上沙滩路，涌进低处的街道，两天都没有退。我们从楼上窗口看着它：先是漫过停车场，风转向后，才慢慢退潮。第三天上午潮水在中午前退去，每堵墙根都留下一道海草，志愿者也一个个从避难所撤了回来。我父亲原本打算今年从救援队退下来，却一直守到最后一条街清理干净。镇上的小店早就经历过一次萧条，如今又要面对付不起的修缮。他说，有些事会倒退，可水只会回到它来的地方。"},"redeem":{"tw":[["redemption",4],["save",0],["pay",2],["repay",6],["deliver",0],["restore",2],["ransom",0]],"en":"My grandfather left me a thin gold watch, and last winter I left it at a pawn shop to pay for a car repair. The owner was kind. He wrote the date on a card, gave me a copy, and promised the shop would save it for six months. I spent the spring paying back the loan in small amounts, and in June I went in to redeem it. He brought it out, restored the worn strap, and delivered it back into my hand without being asked. I said it must feel like a ransom; he laughed and said a watch is never held for ransom, only for a while. It took two more weeks to repay the rest, and the day I did, he seemed as pleased as I was. Redemption, he said, is just a long word for coming back.","zh":"外公留给我一块薄薄的金表，去年冬天我把它留在当铺，换钱修车。店主很和气。他把日期写在卡片上，给了我一张，说店里会替我留六个月。整个春天我一点一点还那笔钱，六月才去把它赎回来。他拿出来，把磨旧的表带修整好，没等我开口就递回我手里。我说这感觉像赎金，他笑了，说表从来不是拿赎金换的，不过是暂放一阵。又过了两周我才把余下的还清，那天他看起来和我一样高兴。他说，赎回不过是个长一点的词，意思是回来。"},"savour":{"tw":[["savoury",4],["taste",2],["season",2],["rejoice",6],["flavour",0],["squander",6],["relish",0]],"en":"My aunt makes a winter soup that fills the whole house with its savour before anyone sits down. She starts with onions and a little bacon, adds a savoury bone, and lets it go for three hours on the lowest flame. The seasoning is the part she never measures: a pinch of pepper, a bay leaf, and salt added at the end. When we finally eat, she tells us to slow down and relish the first spoonful, because the flavour changes as it cools. Last year my cousin, in a hurry, squandered his bowl by eating it in two minutes and then asked for more. My aunt refused and told him to sit and rejoice in the smell at least. He did, and the second bowl, he admitted, tasted twice as good.","zh":"姑妈做的冬日汤，人还没坐下，香味就已经飘满整间屋子。她先下洋葱和一点咸肉，加一根带油香的大骨，用最小的火煨上三个钟头。调味是她从不计量的部分：一撮胡椒、一片月桂叶，盐留到最后才放。真到吃的时候，她让我们慢一点，细细品味第一勺，因为汤一凉，味道就变了。去年表弟赶时间，两分钟就把一碗喝完，白白糟蹋了，然后又来要。姑妈不给他添，让他至少坐下来好好闻闻那股香气。他照做了，第二碗，他承认，好吃了一倍。"},"subvert":{"tw":[["subversive",4],["undermine",0],["destroy",2],["defame",6],["overthrow",0],["instigate",6],["counteract",0]],"en":"The choir had sung the same four pieces for years, and when a new conductor arrived she wanted to subvert the whole arrangement. She moved the altos to the front, cut the organ solos, and asked us to learn a piece nobody knew. Two senior members saw this as a subversive plot to undermine their authority, and one of them began to spread a story that the conductor had been hired to destroy the group. It was a quiet attempt to defame her, and it nearly worked. At the next meeting she did not argue; she let us sing, and the sound answered for her. The old system was not easy to overthrow in one evening, but the talk changed. She said a good choir must counteract habit, and that you cannot instigate change by shouting at people.","zh":"合唱团多年来只唱那四支曲子，新指挥来了以后，想把这套安排整个掀翻。她把女低音调到前排，砍掉管风琴独奏，还让我们学一支谁都没听过的曲子。两位老团员把这看成一场要削弱他们威信的颠覆性阴谋，其中一个开始散布说法，说这位指挥是请来毁掉合唱团的。那是一场安静的抹黑，差点就奏了效。下一次排练她并没有争辩，只是让我们唱，声音替她作了回答。老规矩不是一晚就被推翻的，但大家的话头变了。她说，一个好的合唱团必须对抗惯性，而你不能靠冲人喊叫来策动改变。"},"vitality":{"tw":[["vital",4],["energy",2],["juice",3],["force",2],["dynamism",6],["sparkle",3],["resilience",6]],"en":"My grandmother is eighty-eight and still keeps a garden that would tire a person half her age. She is up before six, drinks a glass of orange juice on the step, and is digging by seven. Visitors always remark on her vitality: the energy in her hands, the force of her laugh, the sparkle in her eyes when she talks about next year's beans. She says the dynamism of a garden is what keeps her going, and that a body is like soil, which needs turning. Two winters ago she broke a hip, and the doctors were amazed by her resilience; she was walking the path again by March. Her life is not easy, but it is vital in a way that has nothing to do with age.","zh":"我奶奶八十八岁了，还打理着一片能让年纪只有她一半的人累垮的园子。她六点前就起床，坐在台阶上喝一杯橙汁，七点已经在翻土了。来做客的人总要说起她的活力：手上的劲、笑声的力道、讲起明年豆子时眼里的亮光。她说园子那种生生不息的劲头推着她往前走，人也像土，得翻一翻。两年前的冬天她摔断了髋骨，医生都惊讶于她的韧性；到三月她又在小路上走了。她的日子并不轻松，可那种生命力，跟年龄毫无关系。"},"reap":{"tw":[["draw",0],["cut",3],["gather",2],["squander",6],["harvest",0],["derive",2],["outlay",6],["glean",0]],"en":"My grandmother planted a strip of maize along the fence in April, and by September we were ready to reap what that small outlay had bought. The harvest took two mornings: we would cut the stalks, gather the cobs into baskets, and glean whatever the birds had left on the ground. She let me draw the water from the well while she worked, and told me how much a farmer derives from patience and a good almanac. A cousin who had squandered his seed money on a motorbike came to help, and nobody mentioned it. At noon we sat in the shade and ate the first boiled corn, which tasted better than anything from a shop. The field looked bare and honest when we finished, and she said the real crop was the lesson.","zh":"四月里奶奶沿着篱笆种了一溜玉米，到九月我们就可以收割那点本钱换来的东西了。收成花了两个早晨：砍下秸秆，把玉米棒收进筐里，再捡起鸟雀落在地里的剩穗。她让我在她干活时从井里打水，还跟我讲，农人能从一个好节气和耐心里得到多少。一个把买种子的钱挥霍在摩托车上的表哥也来帮忙，谁都没提这事。中午我们坐在树荫下啃第一锅煮玉米，比店里买的任何东西都香。收完之后地里空得干干净净，她说，真正的收成是这一课。"},"relegate":{"tw":[["submit",2],["reduce",3],["expel",2],["consign",6],["classify",0],["sideline",3],["impel",6],["banish",0]],"en":"When the club signed two younger players, the coach relegated the oldest defender to the reserve team, and the decision split the dressing room. He had been asked to submit a written request before any transfer, but nobody had told him he would be sidelined for the whole season. Reporters were quick to classify him as finished, and one column even called for him to be expelled from the squad. He did not complain; he consigned his anger to a notebook and trained with the reserves every morning. What impelled him was not pride but a promise to his daughter that he would leave on his own terms. In May the first-choice defender was injured, and the old man played the last four games. Nothing banishes experience, he said afterwards, and the club reduced his farewell to a handshake.","zh":"俱乐部签下两名年轻球员后，教练把年纪最大的后卫降到了预备队，这个决定让更衣室分了派。此前有人要求他转队前必须提交书面申请，却没人告诉他整个赛季都会被打入冷板凳。记者们很快把他归为过气球员，有个专栏甚至呼吁把他清出球队。他没有抱怨，只把怒气记在本子里，每天早上跟预备队一起训练。驱使他坚持下去的不是自尊，而是他答应过女儿要按自己的方式离开。五月，主力后卫受伤，这位老将打了最后四场。后来他说，经验是赶不走的；俱乐部把他的告别简化成了一次握手。"},"repeal":{"tw":[["lift",0],["recall",3],["cancel",2],["abolish",6],["reverse",0],["renege",3],["provision",6],["revoke",0]],"en":"The residents' committee spent a year fighting for the repeal of a rule that banned bicycles from the courtyard. The provision had been written decades earlier, when the yard was used for drying grain, and it made no sense now. At the first meeting the chair refused to lift the ban, then reneged on a promise to bring the matter to a vote. We collected signatures, and a lawyer explained how a council can abolish an old rule and revoke the permits attached to it. When the vote came, the result was a clear reverse of the earlier decision, and the committee had to cancel the fines already issued. Nobody recalled a happier evening in that hall. The next morning twelve bicycles stood under the window, and the yard felt larger.","zh":"业主委员会花了一年时间，争取废止一条禁止在院子里停放自行车的旧规定。这条款是几十年前写下的，那时院子还用来晒粮食，如今毫无道理。第一次会议上，主席拒绝解除禁令，随后又违背了把事情提交表决的承诺。我们征集签名，一位律师讲解了议事会可以怎样废除旧规、撤销挂在它下面的许可。表决那天，结果与先前的决定正好相反，委员会不得不取消已经开出的罚单。没人记得那个礼堂有过更痛快的夜晚。第二天早上，窗下停了十二辆自行车，院子显得大了许多。"},"retard":{"tw":[["check",0],["stay",3],["change",2],["stagnate",6],["slow",3],["decrease",2],["rejuvenate",6],["delay",3]],"en":"The paint we used on the old metal gate came with a warning that cold weather would retard drying, so we watched the forecast like farmers. My father wanted to stay the work until the weekend, but the rust had already begun to check the hinges, and every extra week would only delay the repair. We covered the gate with a tarp to keep the night air off and to slow the chemical change. In the morning one panel was still soft, so we left it and painted the rest. The garden behind the gate had begun to stagnate after two dry summers, and my mother used the waiting time to rejuvenate the beds with compost. The rust did decrease, bit by bit, under the new coat. Some things cannot be hurried; they can only be protected while they set.","zh":"我们给旧铁门上漆用的那种漆标着警告：天冷会延缓干燥，于是我们像农人一样天天看天气预报。父亲想把活儿推到周末，可锈已经开始侵蚀合页，每多拖一周只会让修理更晚。我们用防水布把门盖住，挡住夜里的凉气，也减慢那场化学变化。第二天早上有一块面板还是软的，我们就先放着，把其余的刷完。门后的园子经过两个旱夏已经开始荒滞，母亲就利用等待的时间用堆肥让花圃重新活过来。在新漆下面，锈确实一点一点少了。有些事急不来，只能在它定型的时候好好护着。"},"rouse":{"tw":[["charge",0],["bother",3],["change",2],["whittle",6],["excite",0],["call",3],["move",2],["wake",0]],"en":"The news that the old cinema would be demolished was enough to rouse the whole street. A retired teacher began to call her neighbours one by one, and within a week the empty hall was full of people who had not spoken in years. The plan did excite the younger families, who had never seen the place open, but losing the parking space would bother the shopkeepers. We had to whittle a long list of demands down to three clear points before the council would listen. On the night of the meeting the chair's speech began to move even the doubters, and someone in the back row stood up to charge the committee with doing nothing. That single challenge was enough to wake the room. I went home tired and hopeful, certain that a town can change once its people are roused.","zh":"老电影院要拆的消息，足以把整条街的人都惊动起来。一位退休教师开始挨个给邻居打电话，不到一周，空荡荡的礼堂里坐满了多年没说过话的人。这个计划确实让年轻家庭兴奋，他们从没见过那地方开门；可失去停车位会让店主们不高兴。我们只好把一长串要求削减到三条清楚的意见，议事会才肯听。开会那晚，主席的发言开始打动连怀疑的人也动容，后排有人站起来指责委员会无所作为。就是这一句把全场唤醒了。我回家时又累又充满希望，确信只要人被唤醒，一个镇子就能改变。"},"sift":{"tw":[["strain",0],["rice",3],["choose",2],["scatter",6],["sieve",4],["riddle",3],["travel",2],["scrape",6]],"en":"Before the wedding feast my aunt put me in charge of the rice, which meant I had to sift three large bags for small stones. I used an old sieve and then a finer riddle, shaking each handful until the dust fell through and only the clean grains stayed. It was slow work, and I learned to choose the good grains by feel as much as by sight. My cousin would scatter a few on a cloth to dry, and the cloudy water from the first rinse had to be strained twice before it ran clear. The sack had travelled from a farm two valleys away. At the end I scraped the last cup from the bottom, and there were enough stones in the bowl to fill a matchbox. Nobody at the table ever knew.","zh":"婚宴前一天，姑妈把大米交给我负责，也就是说我得把三大袋米筛一遍，挑出小石子。我先用粗筛，再用更细的筛子，每抓一把就晃，直到尘土落下、只剩下干净的米粒。这活儿很慢，我也学会了不光用眼看，还用手感去挑好米。表妹会撒一些在布上晾着，头一遍淘米的水浑得很，得滤两遍才清。那袋米是从两个山谷外的一个农场运来的。最后我把袋底剩下的一杯也刮了出来，碗里的石子够装满一个火柴盒。饭桌上没有一个人知道。"},"stew":{"tw":[["sweat",0],["jug",3],["cook",2],["soup",6],["brood",0],["sulk",2],["lamb",6],["fret",0]],"en":"Every Sunday my father makes a lamb stew that fills the whole building with its smell. He would sweat over the pot for three hours, adding a jug of water whenever the sauce grew too thick, and refusing any help with the cooking. My brother, who had lost his match that afternoon, sat at the table and began to sulk, then to brood over the missed goal in silence. My mother told him not to fret about a game and put a bowl of soup in front of him before the stew was ready. By the second helping he had stopped sulking, and by the third he was arguing about next week's match. That is what a good stew does, she said: it steams the bad mood out of a boy.","zh":"每个星期天，父亲都会炖一锅羊肉，香气灌满整栋楼。他会在锅边忙上三个小时，酱汁一稠就加一罐水，做饭的事谁也不让插手。弟弟那天下午输了比赛，坐在桌边先是生闷气，后来就一声不响地反复想那个没进的球。母亲叫他别为一场球赛烦心，在炖菜好之前先给他端了一碗汤。吃到第二碗时他已经不赌气了，第三碗下肚就开始争下周的比赛。她说，一锅好炖菜就是这个用处：把男孩的坏情绪蒸出去。"},"stoop":{"tw":[["bend",2],["huddle",3],["act",2],["squat",6],["bow",0],["hold",2],["stare",6],["crouch",0]],"en":"The old man who sells chestnuts on the corner has a stoop that makes him look shorter than he is, and he never seems to mind it. He would bend over his brazier, huddle against the wind, and hold his hands so close to the coals that I once thought he would burn them. When a child came near, he would squat down slowly until his eyes were level with hers, and bow his head to listen. I crouched beside him one evening and asked why he never stood up straight. He laughed and said a stoop is not a surrender; it is how a tall man talks to small people. Then he made me stare into the fire until I saw the colours he saw. I have never forgotten that small act of teaching.","zh":"街角卖栗子的老人背有点驼，看上去比实际矮，他自己好像从不在意。他会俯身在炭炉上，缩着肩挡风，双手凑得离炭火那么近，我有一次真怕他烫着。有小孩走近，他就慢慢蹲下去，直到眼睛和孩子齐平，再低下头听。一天傍晚我蹲在他旁边，问他为什么不站直。他笑着说，驼背不是认输，那是一个高个子跟小个子说话的方式。然后他让我一直盯着火看，直到看见他看见的那些颜色。那个小小的教导，我一直没忘。"},"submerge":{"tw":[["overwhelm",0],["dive",3],["cover",2],["deform",6],["drown",0],["flood",2],["starve",6],["inundate",0]],"en":"The tide came in faster than the fishermen expected, and within an hour it had begun to submerge the lower path along the harbour. Water would cover the steps, then the benches, then the road itself, until the whole quay was inundated. A young diver who had been checking the moorings had to swim for the ladder, and for a minute the current threatened to overwhelm him. The flood also drowned two engines and starved the pumps of fuel, so the boats sat useless for a week. The storm had deformed one wooden hull so badly that the owner said it looked like a bent leaf. I stood on the hill and watched the sea take a place I knew by heart. Nobody was hurt, but the harbour has never quite looked the same to me.","zh":"潮水涨得比渔民预料的快，不到一小时就开始淹没港口边那条低处的路。水先盖过台阶，再盖过长凳，最后连路面也淹了，整个码头都泡在水里。一个正在检查系泊的年轻潜水员只好游向梯子，有那么一分钟水流几乎要把他吞没。洪水还淹坏了两台发动机，油料进不了泵，船只好搁了一个星期没法用。风暴把一条木船体扭得变了形，船主说它像一片弯了的叶子。我站在小山上，看着海水吞下我熟悉的地方。没有人受伤，可在我眼里，这个港口再也回不到从前的样子。"},"swarm":{"tw":[["cloud",0],["crawl",3],["crowd",2],["roam",6],["pour",0],["hum",2],["bug",6],["stream",0]],"en":"A swarm of small flies appeared over the bins behind the market one hot afternoon, and by evening it had become a cloud that hung over the whole lane. The insects would crawl over the lids, crowd into the smallest gaps, and hum so loudly that shopkeepers had to raise their voices. Some children came to look, and a few tried to swat them, until an old woman said no bug spray would stop a swarm that size. The flies seemed to roam from bin to bin, then pour across the wall into the yard where the bakery keeps its flour. We covered everything and called the council, but the truck took two days. Meanwhile a thin stream of them followed the drain to the next street. It took hard rain to end it, and the lane smelled of soap for a week.","zh":"一个炎热的下午，市场后面的垃圾桶上方出现了一群小飞虫，到傍晚已经聚成一团乌云，罩住了整条小巷。它们爬过桶盖，挤进最小的缝隙，嗡嗡声大得店主们说话都得提高嗓门。有几个孩子跑来看，还想拍打它们，一位老太太说，这么大的虫群，喷什么药都没用。蝇群似乎从一个桶游荡到另一个桶，然后越过墙，涌进面包店堆面粉的院子。我们把东西都盖起来，打电话给管理处，可清运车两天后才来。这期间一小股顺着排水沟流到了下一条街。最后是一场大雨才了结，那条巷子闻了一个星期的肥皂味。"},"syndicate":{"tw":[["family",0],["trust",3],["form",2],["web",6],["pool",0],["sell",2],["mail",6],["consortium",0]],"en":"When the small daily paper in our town was sold, the buyer turned out to be a syndicate that already owned four other titles in the region. The family that had run it for three generations had little choice: the press was old, and the paper could not compete with the free news on every phone. The new owners formed a consortium with two radio stations, and within a month they had pooled their advertising staff into one office. What looked like a simple sale soon became a web of contracts, with each title supplying stories to the others by mail and by wire. Critics said the trust would decide what the town was allowed to read; supporters said the paper would have closed otherwise. Six months on, the pages are thinner, the local stories shorter, and nobody is sure who is really in charge.","zh":"我们镇上那份小日报被卖掉时，买主原来是一个已经拥有本地区另外四份报纸的报业集团。经营了三代的家族几乎没有选择：印刷机老了，报纸也拼不过每部手机上的免费新闻。新东家与两家电台组成了一个联合体，不到一个月就把广告人员并到一个办公室。看上去简单的一次出售很快变成一张合同织成的网，各家报纸靠邮递和线路互相供稿。批评者说，这个托拉斯将决定小镇能读到什么；支持者说，不卖报纸早就关门了。半年过去，版面变薄了，本地新闻变短了，谁也说不清到底是谁在掌舵。"},"taunt":{"tw":[["ride",0],["kid",3],["mock",2],["bully",6],["tease",0],["jeer",3],["scold",6],["rally",0]],"en":"The taunts started on the first day of term, when a group of older boys decided that the quiet newcomer was worth mocking. They would kid him about his shoes, ride him about his accent, and jeer every time he answered a question in class. He pretended not to hear, but the taunt about his father's job clearly hurt more than the rest. A girl in his class, who had been bullied herself the year before, decided to rally the others. She did not scold the boys in front of everyone; she sat beside him at lunch and asked him to join the football team. Within a month he had friends, and the teasing had nowhere to stand. The bullies moved on to someone else, which is what bullies do.","zh":"嘲弄从开学第一天就开始了，几个高年级男生认定这个安静的新同学值得取笑。他们拿他的鞋子开玩笑，拿他的口音打趣，他一在课上回答问题就起哄。他装作没听见，可那句拿他父亲工作取笑的话，显然比别的更伤人。班里一个女孩去年自己也被人欺负过，她决定把大家召集起来。她没有当众训那几个男生，而是在午饭时坐到他旁边，邀他加入足球队。不到一个月他有了朋友，那些嘲笑也就无处落脚了。欺负人的人转而去对付别人，他们向来如此。"},"thrash":{"tw":[["bat",0],["whip",3],["beat",2],["punk",6],["toss",0],["dance",2],["funk",6],["lick",0]],"en":"The final of the village boxing night was less a match than a thrashing. The younger fighter came out determined to beat his opponent quickly, and for two rounds he did: he would whip in a left, bat him back, toss him against the ropes, and dance away before the reply arrived. The older man, written off as a punk by the crowd, took the punishment without complaint. Then in the third round he found his range and began to thrash the boy's ribs with short, heavy punches. The crowd, which had come for a quick win, went into a kind of funk and then began to cheer for the old man. He did not lick him, exactly, but he won clearly on points. Afterwards the two of them sat on the same bench, too tired to speak.","zh":"村办拳击赛的决赛不像是场比赛，更像一场痛打。年轻拳手一上场就想快点解决对手，前两个回合也确实做到了：左手抽进去，右手把他打得撞上围绳，然后在回击到来之前跳开。那位上了年纪的拳手被观众当成没用的老家伙，却一声不吭地挨着。到第三回合，他找到了距离，开始用短而重的拳猛击年轻人的肋部。本来只想看一场速胜的观众先是一阵发慌，随后开始为老人叫好。他并没有真把对方打趴下，但靠点数赢得清清楚楚。赛后两人坐在同一条长凳上，累得说不出话。"},"thwart":{"tw":[["cross",0],["ruin",3],["prevent",2],["derail",6],["frustrate",0],["dash",3],["attempt",6],["spoil",0]],"en":"For two years my aunt tried to open a small bakery in the empty shop on the corner, and everything that could thwart her did. The first attempt failed when the bank withdrew its offer, which seemed to dash her plan completely. Then a neighbour's objection helped to derail the planning application, and a broken water main went on to ruin the floor she had just laid. She refused to let the delays frustrate her and simply waited, crossing out one date after another in her notebook. The council had done little to prevent the mess, but it did finally grant the permit. On opening day the rain threatened to spoil the bread she had set out, so we carried everything inside. She sold out by noon anyway.","zh":"姑妈想在街角那间空铺子里开一家小面包房，折腾了两年，凡是能阻挠她的事都来了。第一次尝试因银行撤回贷款而失败，那几乎把她的计划彻底打灭。接着一位邻居的反对让规划申请偏离了轨道，一根爆裂的水管又把她刚铺好的地面泡坏。她不肯让这些延误把自己逼退，只是等着，在笔记本上把一个又一个日期划掉。管理部门几乎没能防止这一团乱，但最后还是批了许可。开业那天，雨眼看要毁掉她摆出来的面包，我们就把东西全搬进屋里。结果她中午前就卖光了。"},"trash":{"tw":[["glass",0],["litter",3],["waste",2],["garbage",6],["ice",0],["discard",2],["pile",6],["rubbish",0]],"en":"The morning after the festival, the field behind the school was covered in trash. There were paper cups in the grass, a pile of plastic chairs by the gate, and broken glass near the path where someone had dropped a bottle. Volunteers came with sacks and gloves to pick up the litter, and a truck took the rubbish away in three loads. It was depressing to see how much had been discarded in a single night, and how little of it would ever break down. My sister found a bag of ice still half full and a box of food waste that had begun to smell. We worked until noon, and the field looked like a field again. The real garbage, someone said, was not on the ground but in the habit that put it there.","zh":"节日过后的那个早晨，学校后面的空地上一片垃圾。草丛里是纸杯，门口堆着一摞塑料椅，小路旁还有碎玻璃，是谁摔碎了一个瓶子。义工们带着袋子和手套来捡拾杂物，一辆卡车分三趟把垃圾运走。看到一夜之间丢下这么多东西，而其中几乎没有几样会自然分解，让人心里发沉。妹妹找到一个还剩一半的冰袋，还有一箱已经开始发臭的厨余。我们干到中午，空地又像一块空地了。有人说，真正的垃圾不在地方上，而在把它丢下的那个习惯里。"},"twine":{"tw":[["roll",0],["ball",3],["move",2],["thread",6],["wind",0],["clue",3],["mesh",6],["string",0]],"en":"In the garden shed there is a shelf where my grandfather kept every piece of twine he ever saved. It came in a fat ball at first, then in smaller rolls that he would wind around two fingers before tying up a tomato plant. He used string for almost everything: a thread to mark a row, a mesh over the berries, a temporary handle on a bucket. I once asked why he did not simply buy a new roll, and he said the old twine still had a use, which was his way of explaining most things. When the shed was cleaned out last spring, we found no clue as to what half of it had been for. I kept one small ball and a length of the coarse twine, and it still moves in my hand like something alive.","zh":"花园的工具棚里有一层架子，爷爷把每一截麻线都留着。最初是一大团，后来变成一个个小卷，他要把它们绕在两根手指上，才去绑番茄苗。绳子在他手里几乎无所不能：拉一条线来标出行距，给浆果罩上一层网，给水桶做个临时提手。我曾问他为什么不干脆买一卷新的，他说旧麻线还有用处——他解释大多数事情都是这个说法。去年春天清理棚子时，我们根本弄不清其中一多半是做什么用的。我留下一小团和一段粗麻线，它在我手里仍然像活的东西一样动。"},"wane":{"tw":[["decline",2],["drop",3],["decrease",2],["wax",1],["dwindle",6],["ebb",0],["dip",3],["falter",6]],"en":"The fishing trade in our town has been on the wane for as long as I can remember. Twenty years ago there were nine boats; now there are three, and the catch continues to decrease every season. Old men at the harbour say the decline began when the big ships came, and that the numbers will dwindle until nothing is left. Some weeks the price will dip, then recover, then drop again, and nobody can plan ahead. The trade does not wax and wane with the moon any more; it only ebbs. Even the market, which once opened at four in the morning, has begun to falter, with two stalls closed last winter. My uncle still goes out, though his hope has thinned to nothing. He says a man cannot stop just because the tide is going out.","zh":"我们镇上的渔业一直在衰落，从我记事起就是如此。二十年前有九条船，如今只剩三条，捕获量还在一季一季地减少。港口的老人说，衰退是从大船开进同一片海面时开始的，数量会一直缩减到什么都不剩。有些星期鱼价会跌一下，再回升，然后又落下去，谁也没法提前打算。这行当不再随月亮盈亏，只是一味退潮。就连曾经凌晨四点开市的鱼市也开始撑不住，去年冬天关了两个摊位。叔叔还出海，尽管他的希望已经薄得几乎没有。他说，人不能因为潮水退了就停下。"},"warp":{"tw":[["distort",0],["lift",3],["thread",2],["overdrive",6],["buckle",0],["mangle",3],["synchronize",6],["heave",0]],"en":"The old wooden door at the back of the workshop had warped so badly that it would no longer close. Damp had got into the frame over the winter, and the grain had begun to buckle outward along the bottom edge. We had to heave it off its hinges, and the panel seemed to distort as we lifted it, like a page held too close to a fire. My boss wanted to mangle the edges flat with a press, but the carpenter said that would only overdrive the wood and make the warp worse. Instead he set it on two blocks, ran a thread from corner to corner to check the line, and left it to dry for a week. Afterwards the door had to be planed and the latch re-hung before the two halves would synchronize again. It closes now, though it still complains.","zh":"作坊后面那扇旧木门变形得厉害，已经关不上了。冬天潮气进了门框，木纹沿着下缘往外鼓。我们只好把它从合页上抬下来，抬的时候整块门板好像都在扭曲，像一页凑得太近火的书。老板想用压机把边压平，木匠说那只会把木头压过头，让变形更糟。他把门搁在两个木墩上，从一角到另一角拉了一根线来校准，然后放着晾了一个星期。之后还得刨平、重装门闩，两扇才能重新对齐。现在它能关上了，只是关的时候还会响。"},"refrain":{"tw":[["chorus",0],["fast",3],["act",1],["urge",6],["abstain",3],["leave",3],["engage",6]],"en":"The school choir has been preparing an old song for the spring concert, and its refrain is the part everyone remembers. The chorus comes in after the second verse, and the same four lines return three times, each one a little louder. Our teacher would urge us to hold the last note, then let the hall act as the instrument. One boy in the back row cannot abstain from tapping his foot, and the sound carries into the quiet bars. We rehearse fast at first, then slow, then at the speed of a walking person. When we finally leave the hall, the refrain stays in my head for hours. On the night itself, the audience began to engage, humming the line before we reached it.","zh":"校合唱团一直在为春季音乐会练一首老歌，它的叠句是人人记得的那一段。副歌唱在第二段之后进来，同样的四句要回来三次，一次比一次响。老师总催我们把最后一个音托住，再让大厅本身当乐器。后排有个男孩总忍不住用脚打拍子，那点声音会钻进安静的几小节里。我们先练快的，再练慢的，最后按一个人走路的节奏来。排练结束离开大厅时，那句叠句在我脑子里转了好几个小时。演出那晚，观众也跟着投入进来，我们还没唱到，他们已在低声哼那一句。"},"refund":{"tw":[["return",0],["restore",3],["pay",2],["receipt",6],["repay",0],["reimburse",3],["withhold",6]],"en":"The kettle I bought in January stopped working after three weeks, so I took it back to the shop with the receipt folded in my pocket. The assistant tested it, agreed it was faulty, and said I could have a refund or a new one. I asked her to return the money to my card, since I had already bought another. She said the shop would repay me within five days and would reimburse the delivery charge too. It took a week, and I had to call twice, but the sum finally appeared. The manager explained that they withhold nothing when a product fails that early. I did not have to pay anything for the second call, and I was ready to argue, but instead we talked about kettles for ten minutes. She even offered to restore my old one as a spare.","zh":"我一月买的那个水壶用了三周就坏了，于是我把它拿回店里，收据折好放在口袋里。店员试了试，承认是次品，说可以退款，也可以换一个新的。我请她把钱退回到卡上，因为我已经另买了一个。她说店里五天内会把钱还给我，运费也会一并退还。前后花了一个星期，我打了两次电话，钱总算到账了。经理解释说，产品坏得这么早，他们不会扣下任何款项。第二个电话不用我付钱，我本来准备好要争几句，结果我们聊了十分钟水壶。她甚至提出把我那只旧的修好，当备用。"},"reiterate":{"tw":[["repeat",0],["quote",3],["tell",2],["emphasise",6],["iterate",0],["paraphrase",3],["restate",0]],"en":"At the morning safety meeting, the supervisor had to reiterate the same three rules she had given us on Monday. She would repeat them slowly, quote the accident report word for word, then paraphrase the key line in plain language. 'I have to iterate this until it is boring,' she said, 'because boring is what keeps hands whole.' She asked an older worker to restate what he had heard, and he told the room the rule about gloves in his own words. What she wanted to emphasise was not the fine, but the habit. Nobody argued, and the shift began quietly. By Friday the gloves were on before anyone reached the machine.","zh":"早上的安全例会上，主管不得不把我们周一就听过的三条规矩再重申一遍。她慢慢地重复，一字不差地引用事故报告，再用大白话把关键那句解释一遍。她说：'我得把这事反复讲到让人厌烦，因为让人厌烦的东西才能保住一双手。'她请一位老工人复述他听到的内容，他就用自己的话把戴手套那条讲给全车间听。她真正想强调的是习惯，而不是罚款。没人争辩，这一班安安静静地开工了。到周五，大家还没走到机器前就已经戴好了手套。"},"revert":{"tw":[["return",0],["recover",3],["mutate",2],["dictate",6],["regress",0],["relapse",3],["modify",6]],"en":"Behind the house my grandfather's vegetable plot has begun to revert to whatever it was before he farmed it. Nobody has touched it for two years, and the neat beds have regressed into a meadow of tall grass. Brambles return first, then saplings, and the hedge has started to recover its old thickness. He says a garden left alone will mutate into a small wood, and that the soil, not the gardener, will dictate what survives. We could modify the edge with a mower, but he refuses. He had a relapse last winter, and the work is too much now. Still, he walks the path each week, watching the wild come back. 'It is not a loss,' he told me, 'only the land going home.'","zh":"屋后爷爷的菜园正慢慢变回他开垦之前的样子。已经两年没人打理，整齐的菜畦退化成一片高高的野草。先回来的是黑莓藤，然后是幼树，树篱也重新长得又厚又密。他说一块地放任不管就会变成一小片林子，决定什么能活下来的是土壤，不是园丁。我们本可以用割草机把边缘修一修，可他不肯。去年冬天他病又犯了一次，如今这些活实在做不动了。不过他还是每周沿着小路走一趟，看着野性一点点回来。'这不算失去，'他对我说，'这只是土地回家了。'"},"salvage":{"tw":[["save",0],["gather",2],["ditch",6],["relieve",0],["property",2],["wreck",6],["scavenge",0]],"en":"After the river rose into the lower street, the family spent two days trying to salvage what they could from the mud. Neighbours came to help gather photographs, papers and a few dry chairs, and anything too heavy to carry was left in the ditch behind the wall. The house itself was a wreck: one wall gone, the floor lifted, the kitchen pressed into the garden. Nobody could save the piano. A man from the next street came to scavenge old timber for firewood, and they let him take it, which seemed to relieve them both. By evening the salvaged things filled one small room, and the family sat among them with tea. 'Property can be counted later,' the mother said, 'today we only count people.'","zh":"河水漫进下街以后，这家人花了两天时间，想从淤泥里抢救出还能救的东西。邻居们过来帮忙把照片、证件和几把还干的椅子收拢起来，太重搬不动的就留在墙后的沟里。房子本身已经毁得不成样子：一面墙没了，地板翘起，厨房塌进了院子。那架钢琴谁也救不回来。隔街的一个男人来捡旧木料当柴烧，他们让他拿走了，这似乎让双方都松了口气。到了傍晚，抢救出来的东西堆满了一间小屋，一家人坐在其中喝茶。'财物以后再算，'母亲说，'今天我们只数人。'"},"scant":{"tw":[["light",0],["work",2],["tangible",6],["short",0],["supply",2],["hint",6],["stint",0]],"en":"In the week before the holiday, the little shop on our corner had only a scant supply of flour, and the shelves looked strangely light. The owner said the delivery had been short again, and that a stint of three bags per customer was the best she could do. There was no tangible reason for the shortage, only a hint in the shipping notices that something had gone wrong far away. My mother made the shortage work for her: she bought one bag and decided to bake less. 'A thin shelf teaches you what you actually need,' she said. Two days later the truck arrived, and the panic was forgotten. The whole street had eaten less bread that week and complained about it more.","zh":"节前那一周，我们街角的小店面粉存货少得可怜，货架看着空得奇怪。店主说这次送货又短了，每人限购三袋已经是她能做的极限。缺货并没有说得清的原因，只有货运单上的一点暗示，说明很远的地方出了岔子。我母亲倒把这点短缺利用了起来：只买了一袋，决定少烤一些。'货架空一点，才看得清自己真正需要什么，'她说。两天后货车来了，那阵慌张也就被忘了。那一周整条街吃的面包都少了，抱怨却更多了。"},"screech":{"tw":[["scream",0],["cry",2],["weasel",6],["creak",0],["noise",2],["growl",6],["shriek",0]],"en":"Coming down the hill in the rain, the old bus began to screech at every bend, and the sound set my teeth on edge. It was not a scream exactly, more a long metallic cry that seemed to come from the wheels themselves. The driver said the pads were worn, and that we would hear a creak from the door as well, which we did at the next stop. A child in the back let out a shriek at the noise, half laughing, and her mother told her to hush. Somewhere under the floor a low growl started whenever the engine pulled. I watched a weasel cross the road ahead, quick and low, and the brakes cried out again. We reached the bottom safely, but nobody spoke until the bus stopped.","zh":"雨中下坡时，那辆旧公交每过一个弯就开始尖叫，那声音听得我牙根发酸。说不上是真正的尖叫，更像一声长长的金属哭喊，仿佛是从轮子本身发出来的。司机说刹车片磨薄了，还说车门也会吱呀一声，下一站果然如此。后座一个孩子听见这噪声发出一声尖呼，半是笑，她母亲让她别出声。发动机一用力，脚下某处就响起低低的咆哮。我看见一只黄鼠狼从前方路面飞快地低身穿过，刹车又叫了一声。我们平安到了坡底，可直到车停下都没人说话。"},"scroll":{"tw":[["roll",0],["move",2],["thread",6],["curl",0],["manuscript",2],["pencil",6],["coil",0]],"en":"The archivist brought out a scroll that had been rolled in linen for eighty years and let it curl open on the table. It was a manuscript of household accounts, written in a small hand, and the paper had begun to coil at the edges. She asked us not to move it much, only to let our eyes travel along each line. I used a pencil to mark the places she pointed at, never touching the paper itself. A loose thread from the linen wrap lay across one column, and she lifted it away with two thin sticks. 'A scroll is a conversation,' she said. 'Someone wrote it, someone kept it, and now we read it.' The room smelled of dust and old glue.","zh":"档案员取出一卷在亚麻布里卷了八十年的卷轴，让它在桌上慢慢舒展开。那是一份家用账目的手稿，字迹很小，纸边已经开始打卷。她让我们别多动它，只用眼睛顺着每一行往下走。我用铅笔标出她指的地方，从不碰纸面。亚麻包布上脱落的一根线横跨一列字，她用两根细签把它挑开。'一卷卷轴就是一场对话，'她说，'有人写下，有人保存，现在轮到我们读。'屋里满是灰尘和陈年浆糊的气味。"},"shriek":{"tw":[["pipe",0],["cry",2],["giggle",6],["scream",0],["noise",2],["growl",6],["screech",0]],"en":"The kettle in our kitchen has a pipe that begins to shriek long before the water boils, and every morning it wakes the house. My sister says it is not a scream but a cry for help, and she answers it with a giggle. Below that, the old fridge adds a growl, and the pipes under the sink add a screech of their own whenever the tap is turned. The noise in that small room is a little orchestra of complaint. Last week the kettle finally broke, and the silence was so strange that we all stood listening to it. My father bought a new one with a soft whistle, and now the mornings feel unfinished. Nobody admits missing the shriek.","zh":"我们厨房那只水壶的壶嘴在水开之前很久就开始尖鸣，每天早上都把全家吵醒。妹妹说那不是尖叫，而是一声求救，她总笑着应它一句。这声音底下，旧冰箱添上低吼，水槽下的水管一开龙头又添上自己的刺耳声。那间小屋里的一片响动，像一支抱怨的小乐队。上周水壶终于坏了，安静得古怪，我们都站着听那片静。父亲买了个只会轻声鸣叫的新壶，如今早晨总觉得少了点什么。谁也不承认自己想念那声尖鸣。"},"shroud":{"tw":[["cover",2],["line",2],["cloak",6],["hide",0],["wrap",2],["veil",6],["sheet",0]],"en":"By four in the afternoon the fog had begun to shroud the whole harbour, and the cranes turned into grey shapes without edges. It seemed to cover everything at once, and the line of the far hill seemed to hide behind a veil of water. A fine sheet of mist lay on the roofs, so thick that the street lamps looked like small moons. Fishermen wrapped themselves in their coats and spoke in lower voices, as if the fog could hear them. Boats came in slowly, feeling for the wall, and a cloak of white moved between them. Nobody complained; the harbour is used to being hidden for a day. By morning the fog had lifted, and the town stood in the sun.","zh":"下午四点，雾已经开始把整个港口裹住，起重机变成没有棱角的灰色影子。它像是一下子盖住了所有东西，远山的轮廓藏在一层水汽后面。屋顶上铺着一层细薄的雾，厚得路灯看起来像一个个小月亮。渔民们裹紧外套，说话都压低了声音，仿佛雾能听见。船只慢慢进港，贴着堤墙摸索，一团白色的披风在船与船之间移动。没人抱怨，这个港口习惯了被藏起来一天。到了早晨雾散了，小镇站在阳光里。"},"skim":{"tw":[["plane",0],["coat",2],["pour",6],["scan",0],["read",2],["scrape",6],["skip",0]],"en":"The report was ninety pages long, and I had one hour to skim it before the seminar. I decided to scan the headings first, skip the tables, and read only the last line of each section. Rain began to pour against the library window, and a plane crossed the low clouds above the car park. My coat hung over the chair, and I used its sleeve to scrape a smear of ink off the page. Somewhere in chapter four a paragraph caught my eye, a thin coat of theory over a very simple fact. 'Skimming is not reading,' my teacher once said, 'but it tells you what deserves to be read.' I closed the report with ten minutes to spare.","zh":"那份报告有九十页，而我在研讨会前只有一个小时把它略读一遍。我决定先扫一遍小标题，跳过表格，每节只读最后一行。雨开始打在图书馆的窗上，一架飞机掠过停车场上方低低的云层。我的外套搭在椅背上，我用袖子把纸上的一抹墨迹刮掉。第四章某处有一段吸引了我的目光——一层薄薄的理论，裹着一个极简单的事实。'略读不是阅读，'老师曾说，'但它能告诉你什么值得去读。'我合上报告时还剩十分钟。"},"slop":{"tw":[["spill",0],["feed",2],["gulp",6],["splash",0],["move",2],["froth",6],["mire",0]],"en":"Every morning my uncle carries a bucket of slop to the pigs, and half of it spills on his boots before he reaches the pen. He pours the feed into the long trough and watches them gulp it down, splashing the boards with a thin froth. The yard outside is a mire after rain, and he moves through it slowly, one careful step at a time. He says a pig will eat almost anything but will not eat dirt, which is why the trough must be cleaned each day. Last winter the pump froze and the whole yard turned to mud. Now he keeps a spare bucket by the gate. It is not a fine life, he says, but it is a warm one.","zh":"每天早上叔叔都提一桶泔水去喂猪，还没走到猪圈，半桶就洒在了靴子上。他把饲料倒进长槽，看它们大口吞下，溅起的细沫挂在木板上。雨后外面的院子成了一片泥沼，他一步一步慢慢地挪过去。他说猪几乎什么都吃，就是不吃脏东西，所以槽每天都必须刷。去年冬天水泵冻住了，整个院子都成了烂泥。现在他在门边多放了一只桶。他说这日子算不上体面，但热乎。"},"slug":{"tw":[["punch",0],["hook",3],["blow",2],["bullet",0],["moon",3],["hit",2],["idle",0]],"en":"The new boy at the gym was a slug at first, and the coach said so without any kindness. He would moon around the ropes, idle between rounds, and let every punch land on his gloves without moving his feet. Then one evening he threw a hook that came at the bag like a bullet, and the whole room went quiet. The coach made him repeat it: same step, same turn, same short blow to the ribs. A hit like that, he said, is worth ten wild swings. By spring the boy had lost the name and kept the hook. He still moves slowly between rounds, but nobody calls him idle now.","zh":"健身房新来的男孩起初是个懒虫，教练说这话时一点情面都不留。他总在围绳边闲荡，一组打完就偷懒，任拳头打在手套上，脚下却不动。后来有一天傍晚，他打出一记勾拳，砸在沙袋上快得像颗子弹，整个房间都静了。教练让他再来一次：同样的上步、同样的转体、同样往肋部去的一记短击。他说这样一下命中，抵得上十次乱挥。到春天，男孩丢掉了那个外号，留下了那记勾拳。组间休息时他动作还是慢，可再没人说他懒。"},"snag":{"tw":[["rent",0],["tree",2],["flurry",6],["tear",0],["obtain",2],["scuttle",6],["rip",0]],"en":"Halfway up the trail my jacket caught on a snag and opened a long rip down the sleeve. It was an old dead tree, grey and sharp, standing at the edge of the path, and its broken branch had torn the cloth cleanly. I tried to obtain a needle from my bag, but the repair kit was in the car. A flurry of snow began while we stood there, and a squirrel scuttled across the rocks with a nut in its mouth. My sister used a pin to close the rent, which held for the rest of the walk. 'A snag is just a tree that stopped being useful,' she said, 'and it still caught you.'","zh":"走到半路，我的外套被一根断枝勾住，袖子上撕开一道长口子。那是一棵枯死的老树，灰白而尖利，立在小路边缘，断枝把布整整齐齐地划开了。我想从包里找根针，可修补包落在车里。我们站在那儿时下起了一阵小雪，一只松鼠叼着坚果从岩石上窜过。妹妹用别针把那道裂口别上，居然撑到了走完。'枯木不过是没用了的树，'她说，'可它照样能钩住你。'"},"snarl":{"tw":[["snap",0],["sound",2],["swerve",6],["mat",0],["talk",2],["clog",6],["tangle",0]],"en":"Traffic clogged the ring road for an hour, and in the car ahead a dog began to snarl at the window. Every few minutes it would snap at the glass, then turn and tangle its leash around the seat. The sound carried through two closed windows, and my sister had to swerve slightly when it lunged at a passing cyclist. The dog's coat was matted with rain, and its owner kept trying to talk it calm. When the road finally cleared, the car pulled away and the snarling stopped. I said a dog like that should not be loose in a car. My sister only shrugged and said the animal was frightened, not cruel.","zh":"环路堵了一个小时，前面那辆车里有只狗开始冲着车窗低声咆哮。每隔几分钟它就朝玻璃一咬，然后转身把牵引绳缠在座椅上。那声音隔着两层关着的窗都听得见，它朝一个路过的骑车人扑去时，妹妹不得不稍稍打了个方向。狗的毛被雨打成一团一团，车主一直想跟它说话让它安静。路终于通了，那辆车开走，咆哮也停了。我说这样的狗不该在车里放开。妹妹只耸耸肩，说那动物是害怕，不是凶。"},"snort":{"tw":[["bird",0],["cry",2],["inhale",6],["hiss",0],["drug",2],["sneeze",6],["hoot",0]],"en":"At the farm gate the old pony greeted us with a snort, then lowered its head to eat again. It would inhale sharply before each snort, as if clearing its nose of dust. A goose hissed at my sister and she stepped back, which made a small bird in the hedge give a sharp cry and fly off. In the barn an owl hooted twice, and the sound was so soft that we almost missed it. The farmer said the pony had a cough and gave it a drug mixed into its feed. Later the animal sneezed twice, shook its mane and seemed better. We walked home with hay in our shoes.","zh":"农场门口那匹老马喷了个响鼻算是打招呼，然后又低头吃草。每次喷鼻之前它都深深吸一口气，像要把鼻子里的灰清出来。一只鹅冲着妹妹嘶叫，她退了一步，篱笆里一只小鸟受惊发出一声尖啼飞走了。谷仓里一只猫头鹰叫了两声，轻得我们差点没听见。农场主说这马有点咳，把药拌在饲料里给它吃了。后来它打了两个喷嚏，甩甩鬃毛，看着好些了。我们鞋里带着干草走回了家。"},"strangle":{"tw":[["choke",0],["kill",2],["suffocate",6],["repress",0],["suffer",2],["restrain",6],["cramp",0]],"en":"Ivy had been let loose on the back wall for twenty years, and now it was beginning to strangle the old pear tree. The vine had wrapped itself around the trunk and was choking the lower branches, so that new leaves could not open and the roots beneath the grass began to suffocate. My father said the tree was suffering and would die within two seasons if we did nothing. He did not want to kill the ivy either, only to restrain it, so he cut a narrow band around the trunk. The work was slow and cramped, and he had to repress his impatience more than once. 'Plants do not hate each other,' he said. 'They just take the light.' By August the pear tree had two new shoots.","zh":"后墙上的常春藤放任生长了二十年，如今开始把那棵老梨树缠死。藤蔓绕住树干，勒住下面的枝条，新叶展不开，草下的根也开始透不过气。父亲说这树在受苦，再不管的话两个季节内就会死。可他也不愿把常春藤弄死，只想把它约束住，于是绕着树干割开一圈。这活又慢又别扭，他不得不一再压下自己的不耐烦。'植物之间并不仇恨，'他说，'它们只是争光。'到八月，梨树长出了两根新枝。"},"swoop":{"tw":[["slide",0],["stoop",3],["move",2],["spree",6],["pounce",0],["descend",2],["lunge",6]],"en":"On the marsh at dusk the swallows began to swoop low over the water, turning together like one animal. They would descend without warning, slide along the surface, then rise again with a small insect in the beak. One bird seemed to stoop straight at my hat before pulling up at the last second. A hawk watched from a post, too heavy to join the spree, and made its own slow lunge at a mouse instead. The whole flock would move as a single shadow, then pounce on a patch of flies and scatter. I stood still until the light went. It was less like watching birds than watching weather.","zh":"黄昏的湿地上，燕子开始贴着水面低低地俯冲，一起转向，像一头动物。它们毫无预兆地降下来，沿着水面滑一段，再叼着一只小虫重新升起。有一只像是直直地朝我的帽子扑来，最后一刻才拉起。一只鹰立在本桩上看着，身子太重，没法加入这场狂欢，只好自己朝一只田鼠慢慢扑了一下。整群鸟像一片影子那样移动，然后扑向一小片飞虫又散开。我站着不动，直到天光散尽。那不像看鸟，更像看天气。"},"vomit":{"tw":[["cat",0],["remedy",2],["slop",6],["sick",0],["reflex",2],["bathe",6],["cast",0]],"en":"Half an hour out of the harbour my nephew turned pale and had to vomit over the rail. A deck hand came with a mop and a bucket of slop, and soon the boards were clean. The sea was not rough, but the smell of the engine was enough, so his body reacted before he could say a word. It is a reflex, the sailor told us, not an illness, and it passes. Our cat, who had come aboard in a basket, was sick too, and we had to bathe her in the small sink. The sailor gave my nephew a remedy from a tin, a bitter powder in warm water. The boy cast one look at the sea, then slept.","zh":"出港半小时后，我侄子脸色发白，不得不趴在栏杆上吐了。一名水手拿着拖把和一桶污水过来，甲板很快擦干净了。海并不算颠，可光是发动机的气味就够了，他的身体比话先作出反应。水手说这是反射，不是病，会过去的。我们的猫装在篮子里一起上了船，也晕得难受，只好在小水槽里给它洗了洗。水手从铁盒里拿出一种药给我侄子，是苦味的粉末，冲在温水里。男孩朝海看了一眼，然后就睡着了。"},"whine":{"tw":[["creak",0],["talk",2],["growl",6],["screech",0],["travel",2],["grunt",6],["squeak",0]],"en":"The old pump in the cellar would whine for a full minute before any water came up, and the sound travelled through the whole house. First came a creak from the pipe, then a low growl, then a squeak that turned into a screech if you opened the tap too fast. My father would grunt as he worked the handle, and the two of us could not talk over the noise. He said the pump was telling us it was tired, and that we should listen. Last month he replaced the washer, and now the whine is shorter, almost polite. I still go down early to start it, when the house is quiet.","zh":"地窖里那台旧水泵总要先哀鸣整整一分钟才有水上来，声音能穿过整座房子。先是水管吱呀一声，接着一阵低吼，然后是一声尖细的吱叫——要是龙头开得太急，就会变成刺耳的尖叫。父亲摇手柄时会闷哼一声，我们俩在噪声里根本说不上话。他说这泵是在告诉我们它累了，我们该听着点。上个月他换了垫圈，如今那哀鸣短了许多，几乎算得上客气。我还是清早下楼去开它，那时屋里最静。"},"whirl":{"tw":[["offer",0],["move",2],["swirl",6],["pass",0],["run",2],["slumber",6],["crack",0]],"en":"At the summer fair the dance floor became a whirl of colour, and I lost my sister somewhere in the middle of it. Men would offer a hand to anyone standing alone, and the room would move in one direction, then another. Skirts began to swirl, the band played the same tune twice, and the hours passed without anyone looking at a clock. I ran into an old neighbour and we talked for ten minutes without hearing a word. By midnight the floor was quieter, and children were in a slumber on the benches outside. A crack in the boards caught my heel as I left, and I laughed at my own clumsiness.","zh":"夏季集市上，舞池转成一片色彩的回旋，我在人群中间把妹妹弄丢了。男人们会向独自站着的人伸出手，整个场子先朝一个方向转，再朝另一个方向转。裙摆开始旋起来，乐队把同一支曲子奏了两遍，几个小时过去，没人看一眼钟。我碰见一位老邻居，聊了十分钟，一个字也没听清。到午夜地板安静下来，孩子们在外面的长凳上睡着了。我离开时鞋跟卡进地板的一道裂缝，我笑自己笨手笨脚。"},"wince":{"tw":[["shrink",0],["move",2],["sneer",6],["flinch",0],["startle",2],["giggle",6],["funk",0]],"en":"The physiotherapist told me to lift my arm slowly, and I could not help but wince at the first inch. I would flinch before each stretch and then shrink back into the chair, which made her giggle. She said the muscle had been in a funk for months and needed to be startled into working again. 'Pain is information,' she said, 'not a warning to stop.' I tried to move without watching her face. A man on the next table gave a small sneer at my complaints, then winced himself a minute later. We ended up laughing together. That night I could lift the kettle with my bad arm for the first time since spring.","zh":"理疗师让我慢慢抬起手臂，刚抬了一寸我就忍不住皱眉咧嘴。每次拉伸之前我都要缩一下，然后往椅子里退，逗得她笑出声。她说这块肌肉消沉了好几个月，得把它惊一下才能重新干活。'疼痛是信息，'她说，'不是叫你停下的警告。'我试着不去看她的脸，只管动。旁边床上的男人对我的抱怨轻蔑地哼了一声，一分钟后自己也疼得直咧嘴。最后我们一起笑了。那天晚上，入春以来第一次，我的伤臂能把水壶提起来了。"},"yearn":{"tw":[["long",0],["die",3],["desire",2],["cherish",6],["pine",0],["forsake",6],["ache",3]],"en":"My grandmother has lived in the small room near the garden for three years now, and she still yearns for her old kitchen. She will long for the smell of the wood stove, and she will pine for the pear tree she planted as a girl. She does not forsake the present: she knows our names, she asks about school, she keeps a photograph of the house on the shelf. But there is an ache in the way she says 'home', and a desire to sit once more at that table. 'I am not sad,' she told me. 'I only miss things that are gone.' Her friends there die one by one, and she attends every funeral. I have learned to cherish her stories.","zh":"奶奶住进花园边那间小屋已经三年了，可她仍然想念她原来的厨房。她会怀念木柴炉的气味，会为少女时亲手栽下的那棵梨树暗自牵挂。她并没有抛下眼前：她记得我们的名字，会问起学校，架子上一直放着那栋房子的照片。只是她说'家'这个字的时候带着一丝隐痛，还有一种想再坐回那张桌边的渴望。'我不难过，'她对我说，'我只是想念已经不在了的东西。'那里的朋友一个个走了，每一场告别她都去。我学会了珍惜她的故事。"},"yen":{"tw":[["pine",0],["die",3],["longing",2],["dollar",6],["ache",0],["yesterday",6],["yearn",0]],"en":"My cousin has a yen for the northern coast and has been saving every dollar she can since last spring. Her savings app died last winter, so she went back to a jar on the shelf and a paper list. She would pine over photographs of the cliffs, and her longing showed in the way she talked about the sea. Every coin that goes in makes the ache a little lighter. 'Yesterday I put in twenty,' she said last week, 'and today I found the cheap bus.' She yearns for the trip the way other people yearn for a new phone. Her friends think it is a small dream. She says a dream you can count in coins is not small.","zh":"表姐对北部海岸有一份痴想，从去年春天起就在攒下每一块钱。她的存钱软件去年冬天坏了，于是又回到架子上那只罐子和一张纸单。她会对着悬崖的照片出神，说起海来的那种向往藏都藏不住。每投进一枚硬币，那份心痒就轻一点。'昨天我存了二十，'她上周说，'今天我就找到了便宜的那班车。'她盼这趟旅行，就像别人盼一部新手机。朋友们觉得这梦想太小。她说，能用硬币数出来的梦，一点也不小。"},"zoom":{"tw":[["soar",0],["billow",3],["rise",2],["lens",6],["surge",0],["travel",2],["projector",6]],"en":"The film club met in the small room behind the library, where the projector threw a nature film onto a white wall. Halfway through, the camera began to zoom in on a hawk above a valley, and the bird seemed to soar without moving its wings. Dust in the beam would billow whenever the door opened, and the picture shook a little. The narrator's voice would rise as the hawk dropped, and a surge of strings came in behind it. On the long lens you could see every feather, and the camera seemed to travel with the bird. Afterwards a student asked how they filmed it. 'Patience,' the teacher said, 'and a very long lens.'","zh":"电影社在图书馆后面的小屋里活动，投影仪把一部自然纪录片投在白墙上。放到一半，镜头开始推近山谷上空的一只鹰，那鸟像是完全不扇翅膀就在高飞。门一开，光柱里的灰尘就翻涌起来，画面也轻轻一抖。鹰俯冲时旁白的声音升高，弦乐随之涌上来。用长焦镜头，每一根羽毛都看得清，摄像机仿佛跟着鸟一起飞。放完后一个学生问这是怎么拍到的。'靠耐心，'老师说，'和一支很长的镜头。'"}}});
